@essay id="p3-kingdom" rh="JESUS, KINGDOM AND POWER · I"
# What he meant by kingdom
drop: Start with the word, because everything turns on it. *Basileia* means kingdom, kingship or reign, and it's the word for the empire that ruled Judea. "The kingdom of God" is the center of his preaching in the first three gospels. Mark's summary is "The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God has come near." Matthew usually says "kingdom of heaven," a reverent way of not saying God, and means the same thing. He wasn't inventing a private spiritual category. He was making a claim about who governs, in a province where saying so got people crucified along the roads.
What he poured into that political word runs against what the word normally carries. The parables describe the kingdom as small, hidden and growing without permission: a mustard seed, yeast in flour, treasure buried in a field, a merchant who sells everything for one pearl. It belongs to the poor. Children, tax collectors and prostitutes go in ahead of the respectable, and it's harder for the rich to enter than for a camel to go through a needle's eye.
Rank is turned over on purpose. When his followers argue about who's greatest he tells them the rulers of the nations lord it over their subjects, "but it shall not be so among you." In John he washes their feet, a slave's work. When a crowd wants to make him king by force he withdraws. He enters Jerusalem on a donkey. INTERPRETATION: Marcus Borg and John Dominic Crossan read the entry as a counter-procession to the Roman governor's arrival with troops at Passover. The charge on the cross, in all four gospels, is "King of the Jews."
CONTESTED: Whether the kingdom is present, future or both. Albert Schweitzer argued in 1906 that Jesus expected the end of the world soon, C. H. Dodd argued in 1935 that the kingdom was already present in his ministry, and most scholars now hold some version of "already and not yet." Luke 17.21 says the kingdom is *entos hymōn*, "within you" or "among you," and the translation turns it from an inner state into a present reality in a community.
q: The rulers of the nations lord it over them. It shall not be so among you. | MATT 20.25–26
side:
chips: NT, KINGDOM
k: Two sayings the Empire needs
stext: "My kingdom is not of this world" (John 18.36). CONTESTED: *Ek tou kosmou toutou* is "from this world," and many read it as a statement about the kingdom's source more than its location. "Render to Caesar" (Mark 12.13–17) answers a trap about Roman tax. He asks whose image is on the coin. CONTESTED: Two spheres, an evasion, or a challenge, since his hearers know whose image human beings bear (Gen 1.27).
marg
see: For TKC, at the end of this part.
/end

@page rh="JESUS, KINGDOM AND POWER · I · WRITING" m="YOURS; TKC: TL; AM"
# What Dominion teaches the kingdom is
empire: The relocation
POSSIBLE: The Temple empties the word. The kingdom becomes an inner condition of the obedient heart or a reward after death, anywhere but here and never a claim on territory. Having emptied it, the Empire claims the word for itself: Dominion is the kingdom come, which makes resisting the state the same as resisting God. The old companion called this the engine of the novel (AM).
/end
prompt: What the Temple teaches the kingdom is, in its own words | fill
prompt: A scene where the same line is said twice, by two people meaning opposite things | fill

@page id="p3-four" rh="JESUS, KINGDOM AND POWER · II" m="MARK 1.22; MARK 11.28; MATT 23; MARK 11.15–17; MARK 1.41–44; MARK 5.25–34; MARK 7.24–30; LUKE 8.1–3; LUKE 10.38–42; MARK 10.17–22; LUKE 4.16–30; FREDRIKSEN; LEVINE, THE MISUNDERSTOOD JEW (2006)"
# Four things he kept doing
rec:
Authority | Crowds say he teaches "as one who had authority, and not as the scribes," and officials keep asking "By what authority are you doing these things?" He argues sabbath practice, calls scribes and Pharisees "blind guides" and "whitewashed tombs," and drives the money changers out of the temple court. **For TKC:** POSSIBLE: unlicensed authority is the crime Dominion can't forgive, since it's the only one that threatens the licensing system itself.
Bodies | He touches a man with a skin disease and takes a dead girl by the hand. The bleeding woman touches him first, without asking, and he commends her faith. INTERPRETATION: The contagion runs the other way. He doesn't become unclean, and they become well. CONTESTED: Whether that amounts to opposing the purity system. Paula Fredriksen and Amy-Jill Levine warn that reading Jesus as against Jewish law is a Christian habit with an ugly history. He sends the healed man to the priest with the offering Moses commanded.
Women | Mary of Bethany sits at his feet in the student's place, and he defends her. Named women fund the movement. A Syrophoenician woman argues with him over healing her daughter, answers his refusal with "even the dogs under the table eat the children's crumbs," and he grants it "for this saying." She wins the exchange. He's anointed by a woman in a room of men who call it waste. No gospel records him telling a woman to be silent.
Money | He tells a rich man to sell everything, and the man goes away grieving. Luke pairs blessings on the poor with woes on the rich and adds the rich fool and the rich man and Lazarus. In his first sermon, at Nazareth, he reads Isaiah 61, "release to the captives… the year of the Lord's favor," which echoes the Jubilee, and says it's fulfilled today. **For TKC:** INTERPRETATION: The most suppressed material in Dominion's scripture is economic more than sexual. Follow the money and you'll find the censorship.
corr: The old companion said the Nazareth room "turns on him" over the year of release. The congregation first speaks well of him (4.22). They try to throw him off a cliff after he says Elijah and Elisha were sent to foreigners, a widow of Sidon and a Syrian general (4.24–30).
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page id="p3-prayer" rh="JESUS, KINGDOM AND POWER · THE LORD'S PRAYER · 1 OF 4" m="MATT 6.7–13; LUKE 11.1–4; P75; CODEX VATICANUS; CODEX SINAITICUS; DIDACHE 8.2"
# Thy kingdom come
The prayer appears twice, in different settings and forms. In Matthew it's part of the Sermon on the Mount, taught in place of heaping up empty phrases. In Luke the disciples ask "Lord, teach us to pray," and he gives a shorter version. Every pronoun for the people praying is plural. There's no "I" in it.
tabrl:
| Clause | Matthew 6.9–13 | Luke 11.2–4 | Common liturgical form |
| Address | Our Father in heaven | Father | Our Father, who art in heaven |
| Name | Hallowed be your name | Hallowed be your name | Hallowed be thy name |
| Kingdom | Your kingdom come | Your kingdom come | Thy kingdom come |
| Will | Your will be done, on earth as it is in heaven | Absent in the earliest manuscripts | Thy will be done, on earth as it is in heaven |
| Bread | Give us this day our daily bread | Give us each day our daily bread | Give us this day our daily bread |
| Forgiveness | Forgive us our debts, as we also have forgiven our debtors | Forgive us our sins, for we ourselves forgive everyone indebted to us | Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us |
| Testing | Lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil (or "the evil one") | Lead us not into temptation | Lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil |
| Doxology | Absent in the earliest manuscripts | Absent | For thine is the kingdom, and the power, and the glory, for ever. Amen. |
corr: The old companion presented one prayer and treated "on earth as in heaven" as part of the title line. "Thy kingdom come" is in both gospels. "On earth as in heaven" is Matthew's alone, the doxology is a later addition, and the prayer most people recite is Matthew's text with a liturgical ending.

@page rh="JESUS, KINGDOM AND POWER · THE LORD'S PRAYER · 2 OF 4" m="KADDISH; EPIOUSIOS; JEROME, VULGATE; TYNDALE (1526); BOOK OF COMMON PRAYER (1549); MATT 6.14–15"
# Clause by clause
rec:
Our Father in heaven | A shared parent makes the people praying siblings, and rank inside the family a fiction. INTERPRETATION: Authority is placed where no earthly office can occupy it. POSSIBLE: Dominion prefers the clause said upward and never sideways.
Hallowed be your name | A request that God's name be treated as holy. INTERPRETATION: It implies no other name is, imperial or dynastic. Ask what Dominion has carved above its doors.
Your kingdom come | The title line, in both gospels. It asks for the kingdom's arrival, which assumes it isn't fully here. The Jewish Kaddish, an Aramaic prayer whose core many date early, also pairs the hallowing of God's name with a prayer that the kingdom come soon, and the two are often compared.
Your will be done, on earth as in heaven | Matthew's. It's absent from Luke in the earliest manuscripts (P75, Codex Vaticanus) and was added by later copyists. INTERPRETATION: In Matthew it ties the kingdom to the earth, to the place where taxes are collected and soldiers are stationed.
Our daily bread | *Epiousios*, "daily," appears nowhere in Greek before the gospels, and its meaning is uncertain: "for today," "for tomorrow," "necessary for existence." Jerome translated it two ways, "supersubstantial" in Matthew and "daily" in Luke. It asks for enough, and for "our" bread more than "my" bread.

@page rh="JESUS, KINGDOM AND POWER · THE LORD'S PRAYER · 2 OF 4, CONTINUED" m="KADDISH; EPIOUSIOS; JEROME, VULGATE; TYNDALE (1526); BOOK OF COMMON PRAYER (1549); MATT 6.14–15"
# Clause by clause, continued
rec:
Forgive us our debts | Matthew says debts, *opheilēmata*. Luke says sins, though his second half keeps the debt language. Both tie the request to the one praying it. "Trespasses" isn't in either gospel's prayer. It comes from Tyndale's Matthew (1526) and the Book of Common Prayer (1549), and passed into Anglican and Catholic English. Matthew's very next verses do use a word for trespasses.
Lead us not into temptation | *Peirasmos* means testing or trial as well as temptation. INTERPRETATION: A request to be spared the test, more than to pass it heroically. Pope Francis criticized the wording in 2017, and the French and Italian Catholic liturgies changed it. "From evil" can also be "from the evil one."
For thine is the kingdom | Absent from the earliest manuscripts of Matthew and from every early Luke. A shorter form appears in the Didache, about 100 CE. Later manuscripts added it, the King James printed it, and most modern translations footnote it.
corr: The old companion said institutions render the clause "trespasses" to blunt its money sense. Debts, sins and trespasses track which text and translation tradition a church inherited, and they aren't evidence of motive. Churches that say "debts," Presbyterian and Reformed ones among them, follow the King James Matthew.

@page rh="JESUS, KINGDOM AND POWER · THE LORD'S PRAYER · 3 OF 4 · DOMINION'S VERSION" m="WRITING; TKC: DR3 · TL; OPEN QUESTION"
# Dominion's version
INFERENCE: The Book of Covenant uses King James wording (DR3), so the Temple's base text is probably Matthew 6 as the KJV prints it: "debts," "Thy will be done in earth, as it is in heaven," and the doxology. OPEN QUESTION: Does the Temple say "debts" or "trespasses" in public worship, and does it print Luke's shorter version anywhere?
prompt: Dominion's official text, line by line. What's cut, what's added | fill
prompt: Who in the cast says "debts" and who says "trespasses," and does anyone notice? | fill

