@opener id="p3-grid" rh="PART III · THE GOSPEL GRID" m="SOURCE MARGIN; MATT · MARK · LUKE · JOHN; NA28 · UBS5; SANDERS, THE HISTORICAL FIGURE OF JESUS (1993); EHRMAN, THE NEW TESTAMENT"
plate: quote
pk: Luke 1.1–3
pq: Many have undertaken to compile a narrative of the things that have been accomplished among us…
pq: it seemed good to me also, having followed all things closely for some time past, to write an orderly account for you.
pr: A gospel that opens by saying there were others. Four, kept side by side.
/end
tk: REFERENCE UNIT · ◈
big: The Gospel Grid
lede: There's no single gospel. There are four, written for different communities decades apart, and they disagree about the order of events, the wording of the sayings, the day of the death and what happened at the tomb. This unit holds all four at once.
calls:
k: Why a grid and not a harmony
A harmony stitches the four into one story and quietly deletes the differences. The differences are the most useful material in the New Testament for a writer. They show four groups deciding what mattered.
||
k: What this unit won't do
Tell you which account is right, whether the differences are contradictions or perspectives, or what any of it obliges you to believe. Every date and source theory is marked as argued.
/end

@page rh="GOSPEL GRID · 1 OF 6 · THE FOUR AT A GLANCE" m="MARK 1.1; MATT 1.1; LUKE 1.1–4; JOHN 1.1; JOHN 20.31; PAPIAS (2ND C.)"
# The record on each
All four are formally anonymous. The names were attached by tradition, generally in the second century. Every date is a mainstream range, and every one is argued.
tabrl:
| | Mark | Matthew | Luke | John |
| Usual date | About 65–75 | About 80–90 | About 80–95 | About 90–110 |
| Length | 16 chapters, the shortest | 28 chapters | 24 chapters, continued in Acts | 21 chapters |
| Opens with | John the Baptist in the wilderness | A genealogy from Abraham | A dedication claiming orderly research | "In the beginning was the Word," before creation |
| Birth story | None | Magi, Herod's massacre, flight to Egypt | Shepherds, Mary's song, the temple | None |
| Genealogy | None | Abraham to Joseph, naming Tamar, Rahab, Ruth and "the wife of Uriah" | Back to Adam, by a different line after David | None |
| Teaching | Short, often private explanations | Five large blocks, the Sermon on the Mount first | The Sermon on the Plain, the most parables | Long first-person discourses, the "I am" sayings |
| Exorcisms | Central | Frequent | Frequent | None |
| Leans toward | Disciples who don't understand. His identity is named at the cross by a Roman soldier. | Fulfillment, the law, community discipline, judgment | The poor, reversals of status, women, foreigners | Who Jesus is, and belief |
prompt: Which of the four voices is closest to how my world tells its own founding story | fill

@page rh="GOSPEL GRID · 2 OF 6 · THE FOUR AT A GLANCE, CONTINUED" m="MARK 15.34; MATT 27.46; LUKE 23.34, 46; JOHN 19.30; MARK 16.8; MARK 16.9–20"
# The record on each, continued
tabrl:
| | Mark | Matthew | Luke | John |
| Lord's Prayer | Absent | 6.9–13 | 11.2–4 | Absent |
| Ministry | Can be read as about a year, one trip to Jerusalem | Same | Same | Three Passovers and several trips, the source of "three years" |
| Temple action | Final week, the trigger for the arrest | Final week | Final week | At the start (ch. 2) |
| Last meal | A Passover meal | A Passover meal | A Passover meal | Before Passover. He dies as the lambs are slaughtered. |
| Last words | "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" then a loud cry | The same cry | "Father, into your hands I commit my spirit" | "It is finished" |
| At the tomb | Three women, the stone rolled away, a young man inside. They flee and tell no one. | Two Marys, an earthquake, an angel, guards. They meet Jesus on the way. | Several named women, two men in dazzling clothes. The apostles think it's nonsense. | Mary Magdalene, then Peter and another disciple. Jesus appears to her first. |
| Ending | The earliest manuscripts end at 16.8 with the women afraid. The longer ending is a later addition. | A commission on a mountain in Galilee | Appearances near Jerusalem, the ascension | Two endings, 20.30–31 and ch. 21 |
corr: The old companion said "no two of the four give the same last words." Mark and Matthew give the same cry, Eloi in Mark and Eli in Matthew. Luke's "Father, forgive them" (23.34) is missing from some of the earliest manuscripts, and its originality is CONTESTED.
prompt: Which ending my world's founding account uses, and who cut the others | fill

@page rh="GOSPEL GRID · 3 OF 6 · WHO COPIED WHOM" m="MARK 8.22–26; MARK 14.51–52; MATT 2; MATT 25.31–46; LUKE 1.46–55; JOHN 11; GOODACRE, THE CASE AGAINST Q (2002)"
# The overlap problem
Matthew, Mark and Luke run parallel closely enough to print in columns, same episodes, same order, sometimes identical Greek for long stretches. Independent memory doesn't do that. Some literary relationship is certain. CONTESTED: Which one.
tabrl:
| Proposal | What it claims | Its weak point |
| Two-source, the majority view | Matthew and Luke each used Mark, plus a lost sayings collection called Q, plus material of their own | Q has never been found and is reconstructed from the texts it explains |
| Farrer · Goodacre (2002) | Luke used Mark and Matthew both, so no lost source is needed | It has to explain why Luke broke up Matthew's sermons and moved the pieces |
| Matthew first · Griesbach | Matthew is earliest, and Mark condensed the others | It has to explain why a condenser would add rough Greek and unflattering details |
| John | Knew the other three, knew a tradition behind them, or wrote independently. A separate dispute. | Its differences are too large to be editorial trimming |
rec:
Only in Mark | A healing that takes two tries, a young man who runs away naked at the arrest, and the abrupt ending at the empty tomb.
Only in Matthew | The magi, the massacre of the children, the guard at the tomb, and the sheep and the goats.
Only in Luke | The shepherds, Mary's song, the good Samaritan, the prodigal son, the rich man and Lazarus, the road to Emmaus. Most of what's quoted about poverty and mercy is his.
Only in John | Cana, Nicodemus at night, the Samaritan woman, Lazarus raised, the foot washing and the long farewell.
box: The finding that matters for TKC
INTERPRETATION: Whatever the order, later writers visibly edited earlier ones, softening, reordering, adding a birth and adding an ending. Scripture inside the canon is visibly revised scripture. An empire that claims a fixed, unedited text is claiming something its own documents contradict, and that's a device as much as a polemic.
/end
prompt: Who edited the founding account in my world, and what they softened | fill

