@page rh="GOSPEL GRID · 5 OF 6 · THE REAL DISAGREEMENTS" m="JOHN 2.13–22; JOHN 19.14–31; MATT 1 · LUKE 3; MATT 5 · LUKE 6; MATT 6 · LUKE 11; MARK 16 · JOHN 20"
# Where they don't match
These are differences in the text. Whether they're contradictions or complementary perspectives is a theological judgment this companion leaves to you. Their being differences isn't in dispute.
rec:
The temple action | In the first three it happens in the final week and triggers the arrest. In John it opens the ministry. Same event, opposite role.
The day of death | The first three make the last meal a Passover meal. John puts the crucifixion before the Passover meal, so the death coincides with the slaughter of the lambs.
The genealogies | Matthew and Luke give different lines with different names between David and Joseph, running in opposite directions. Reconciliations have been proposed, and none commands agreement.
After the birth | Matthew has a flight to Egypt to escape a massacre. Luke has a calm presentation at the temple and a return to Nazareth. The two itineraries are hard to run at once.
The beatitudes | Matthew sets them on a mountain and blesses the poor in spirit. Luke sets them on level ground, blesses the poor, and adds matching woes on the rich. One is interior and one is economic.
The prayer | Luke's is shorter, and the familiar closing line is missing from the oldest manuscripts of both. The prayer most recited in the world is a composite.
At the tomb | The number of women, the number of figures, whether the stone is already moved, who's told, Galilee or Jerusalem, and whether anyone speaks all differ. Women are the first witnesses in every one.
box: The one detail they all keep
INTERPRETATION: Four accounts that agree on almost no logistics all put women at the tomb first, in a culture where women's testimony carried little legal weight. Some scholars argue an inventing community wouldn't have chosen it. It's an argument from plausibility and not a proof.
/end
prompt: Whose testimony my world refuses to count, and what they saw anyway | fill

