@page id="p3-tkc" rh="JESUS, KINGDOM AND POWER · FOR TKC · 1 OF 3" m="TKC: TL · DR3 · TR · DOM · CR · WR · AM"
# The prayer and the Trials
rec:
The title, twice | Elias Vane's 2033 sermon is called Thy Kingdom Come (TL), and the novel takes the same words. INTERPRETATION: Every character who prays the line is asking either for the Empire to last or for something to replace it, and may not know which.
Relocating the kingdom | POSSIBLE: The Temple makes "My kingdom is not of this world" its favorite verse and then names the Empire as the kingdom come. The move needs both halves, the kingdom emptied of territory and the Empire claiming the territory.
On earth as in heaven | POSSIBLE: The clause the Temple can't spiritualize, because it names the earth. Someone who's read Luke knows the prayer works without it, and someone who's read Matthew knows it doesn't.
Debts and levies | In Aurem, families of the top five Assessment finishers get the kind of review where unpaid levies disappear (DR3). INTERPRETATION: The Empire forgives a household's debts in exchange for its daughter. POSSIBLE: A character notices the Temple says "trespasses" in the same building as the levy office.
Lead us not into testing | INTERPRETATION: Twelve Covenant Daughters pray every week to be spared the test while the Empire tests them for four months. POSSIBLE: The Temple teaches the clause as a prayer for the strength to pass.
Women at the tomb | Eden's Theological Interview report runs three pages longer than anyone else's, and the officer can't tell whether she's the most aligned woman in twenty years or the most clear-eyed (TR). POSSIBLE: Whose testimony counts is the Trials' question too.
The Syrophoenician woman | POSSIBLE: The precedent for Eden's Public Testimony, where every sentence is defensible and the whole is incendiary (TR).

@page rh="JESUS, KINGDOM AND POWER · FOR TKC · 2 OF 3" m="TKC: DOM · CR · TR · WR 36 · WR 38–39"
# The procession and the Broadcast
rec:
The donkey and the Boulevard | The Opening Procession gives Aurem's Covenant Daughter forty-seven seconds on Sovereign Boulevard (DOM), and every walk is choreographed (CR). POSSIBLE: Someone knows the entry into Jerusalem was read as a parody of a governor's procession, and walks the Boulevard thinking about it.
Rank turned over | POSSIBLE: Judah knows Mark 10.42–44, and it's part of why he doesn't perform (WR 36). In the Pairing Introduction he sends the officials out (TR), the closest thing in the Trials to washing feet.
Four gospels and one Broadcast | The Broadcast edits the Civic Debates for Dominion release, cutting exchanges and building narratives decided in advance (TR). INTERPRETATION: The gospels show four communities shaping one story, openly and in the canon. The Broadcast does it in secret and calls the result the record.
Keeping the anti-Jewish trope out | The Temple of TKC is the Empire's institution. When Jesus argues with Pharisees and scribes, the argument is inside first-century Judaism. If the novel uses those scenes against the Temple, the narration has to know the difference, or it repeats an old Christian slander (WR 38–39).
Luke 4 | POSSIBLE: The Temple preaches the release of Luke 4.18 as spiritual and drops 4.25–27, since favor to outsiders is what the Empire can't afford in any Dominion.
